{"name":"Sooty Willow Bamboo","hex":"#4d4b3a","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sooty","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, relating to, or producing soot.","Soiled with soot"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To blacken or make dirty with soot."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sooty"},{"term":"willow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various deciduous trees or shrubs in the genus Salix, in the willow family Salicaceae, found primarily on moist soils in cooler zones in the northern hemisphere.","The wood of these trees."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To open and cleanse (cotton, flax, wool, etc.) by means of a willow.","To form a shape or move in a way similar to the long, slender branches of a willow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/willow"},{"term":"bamboo","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the fast-growing grasses of the Bambusoideae subfamily, characterized by its woody, hollow, round, straight, jointed stem.","Wood of the bamboo plant as a material for building, furniture, etc."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of the wood of the bamboo."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bamboo"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sooty","blocks":[{"text":"Sooty is a British children's television media franchise created by Harry Corbett incorporating primarily television and stage shows. The franchise originated with his fictional glove puppet character introduced to television in The Sooty Show in 1955. The main character, Sooty, is a mute yellow bear with black ears and nose, who is kind-hearted but also cheeky. Sooty performs magic tricks and practical jokes, and squirts his handler and other people with his water pistol. The franchise itself also includes several other puppet characters who were created for television, as well as an animated series, two spin-off series for the direct-to-video market, and a selection of toy merchandising."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/44/Sooty_Logo_%281996-present%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sooty"},{"title":"Willow","blocks":[{"text":"Willows, also called sallows and osiers, of the genus Salix, comprise around 350 species of typically deciduous trees and shrubs. They are primarily found on moist soils in cold and temperate regions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Salix_alba_Morton.jpg/330px-Salix_alba_Morton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Willow"},{"title":"Bamboo","blocks":[{"text":"Bamboos are a diverse group of mostly evergreen perennial flowering plants making up the subfamily Bambusoideae of the grass family Poaceae. Giant bamboos are the largest members of the grass family, in the case of Dendrocalamus sinicus having individual stalks (culms) reaching a length of 46 meters (151 ft), up to 36 centimeters (14 in) in diameter and a weight of up to 450 kilograms (1,000 lb). It grows in tropical and subtropical regions, especially in Asia. The internodes of bamboos can also be of great length. Kinabaluchloa wrayi has internodes up to 2.5 meters (8 ft) in length, and Arthrostylidium schomburgkii has internodes up to 5 meters (16 ft) in length, exceeded in length only by papyrus. By contrast, the stalks of the tiny bamboo Raddiella vanessiae of the savannas of French Guiana measure only 10–20 millimeters (0.4–0.8 in) in length by about 2 millimeters (0.08 in) in width. The origin of the word \"bamboo\" is uncertain, but it most likely comes from the Dutch or Portuguese language, which originally borrowed it from Malay."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Bamboo_forest.jpg/330px-Bamboo_forest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bamboo"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/9b/Sooty2011.png/250px-Sooty2011.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/9b/Sooty2011.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/9b/Sooty2011.png/250px-Sooty2011.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The present puppet design of Sooty since 2011","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASooty2011.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Sootypixiephone.jpg/500px-Sootypixiephone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Sootypixiephone.jpg/500px-Sootypixiephone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sooty \"xylophones\" (which are actually glockenspiels)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASootypixiephone.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/fe/Sweep_-_Puppet_Design_2011.jpg/250px-Sweep_-_Puppet_Design_2011.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/fe/Sweep_-_Puppet_Design_2011.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/fe/Sweep_-_Puppet_Design_2011.jpg/250px-Sweep_-_Puppet_Design_2011.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sweep made his debut in 1957 and has remained a part of the franchise.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASweep_-_Puppet_Design_2011.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/a/a2/Soo_and_Sooty.jpg/330px-Soo_and_Sooty.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/a/a2/Soo_and_Sooty.jpg/330px-Soo_and_Sooty.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Soo (left) made her debut in 1964 and has remained a part of the franchise since her first appearance.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASoo_and_Sooty.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Salix_silesiaca_a5.jpg/500px-Salix_silesiaca_a5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Salix_silesiaca_a5.jpg/500px-Salix_silesiaca_a5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"At the base of the petiole, a pair of stipules form. These may fall in spring, or last for much of the summer or even for more than one year (marcescence).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASalix_silesiaca_a5.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Young-catkin.jpg/500px-Young-catkin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Young-catkin.jpg/500px-Young-catkin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Young male catkin","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYoung-catkin.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Bourgoyen_knotted_willow_and_woodpile.jpg/500px-Bourgoyen_knotted_willow_and_woodpile.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Bourgoyen_knotted_willow_and_woodpile.jpg/1280px-Bourgoyen_knotted_willow_and_woodpile.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Bourgoyen_knotted_willow_and_woodpile.jpg/500px-Bourgoyen_kno